@chapter 10 | Money, Inflation and the Reserve Bank of India

@intro
The money and banking section is the largest in the economy paper, so it is split into three chapters. This first one covers **money** (money supply measures, currency, coins, demonetisation), **inflation** (types, who gains, CPI, WPI, core and headline inflation), and the **Reserve Bank of India** (functions, monetary policy, CRR, SLR, repo, reverse repo, bank rate, open market operations and Governors). It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 10.1 | Money Supply and Currency

@q 8:1

@src 8:2
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2009; U.P.P.C.S. (Spl.) (Mains) 2008
Money multiplier in India is defined as :
(a) Broad Money / Base Money
(b) Broad Money / Reserve Money
(c) Reserve Money / Base Money
(d) Base Money / Reserve Money
@ans (b)
@note The **money multiplier** is **broad money (M3) divided by reserve money (M0)**. The source prints each option as a fraction.
@end

@table
Measure | Includes
**M0 (reserve money)** | Currency in circulation + bankers' deposits with RBI + other deposits with RBI
**M1 (narrow money)** | **Currency with the public + demand deposits** + other deposits with RBI
M2 | M1 + savings deposits of post offices
**M3 (broad money)** | **M1 + time deposits** of banks; the most used measure
M4 | M3 + all post office deposits
New aggregates (Y.V. Reddy group, 1998) | NM0-NM3 and liquidity measures L1-L3
@end

@q 8:3-4

**Demonetisation** (8 November **2016**) withdrew the **Rs. 500 and Rs. 1,000** notes. Its stated aims were to fight **black money, fake notes and terror funding**, cut the use of cash, push **digital payments** and widen the tax base; raising GDP growth was not a stated aim. About **99%** of the withdrawn notes came back. The **Rs. 2,000** note (November 2016) was withdrawn from circulation in May **2023**.

@q 8:5-8

@q 8:9-10

@table
Currency fact | Detail
First paper money | Bank of Hindostan (1770); **Government paper currency from 1861-62** (Paper Currency Act, 1861)
**Decimal coinage** | **1 April 1957** (naye paise)
Note issue system | **Minimum Reserve System** (since **1956**): at least **Rs. 200 crore** of gold and foreign exchange, of which **Rs. 115 crore** in gold
RBI's power | Can issue notes up to **Rs. 10,000** (Rs. 10,000 notes were issued in 1938 and 1954 and demonetised in 1946 and 1978)
**Coins and Re. 1 notes** | Issued by the **Government of India** (Ministry of Finance); Re. 1 note bears the **Finance Secretary's** signature
**Mints** | **Mumbai, Kolkata, Hyderabad, Noida** (UP, 1988)
Note presses | Nashik, Dewas (government); Mysuru, Salboni (RBI's BRBNMPL)
Rupee symbol (₹) | 2010, designed by **D. Udaya Kumar**
@end

@section 10.2 | Inflation

@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2017
Which of the following is/are definite implication(s) of a fall in inflation rate :
I. Prices have fallen.
II. Prices are increasing more slowly than before.
III. Food supply has increased.
IV. There is industrial stagnation.
Select the correct answer from the codes given below:
Codes :
(a) I and III
(b) I only
(c) II only
(d) I, III and IV
@ans (c)
@note A **fall in the inflation rate (disinflation)** only means prices are still rising, but more slowly. Prices actually falling is **deflation**.
@end
@src 8:11

@q 8:12-13

@table
Inflation type | Meaning
**Demand-pull** | Too much money chasing too few goods
**Cost-push** | Rising costs (wages, oil) push prices up
**Bottleneck** | **Supply shortages** and poor distribution; hard to tackle with demand tools
**Stagflation** | High inflation + **stagnation** (low growth, high unemployment)
**Disinflation** | Slower rise in prices
**Deflation** | Fall in the general price level
Creeping / galloping / **hyperinflation** | Below 3% / double digits / runaway
@end

**Debtors** gain from inflation (they repay in cheaper money); **creditors, savers and fixed-income earners** (pensioners) lose. To control inflation the government **raises** interest rates, curbs demand and money supply, and may ration goods; **lowering interest rates** would feed inflation.

