@qnote 8:15 | When this was first asked (2004-2008), the **WPI** was India's headline inflation measure, so (b) was the answer then. Since **2014** (Urjit Patel committee) the RBI targets **CPI (Combined)** inflation, and the source gives the present answer, **(c)**.

@q 8:16-19

@q 8:20-23

@table
Index | Compiled by | Base | Use
**CPI (Combined)** | **NSO, MoSPI** | 2012 (being revised to a newer base) | **Headline inflation**; RBI's target (goods and services)
**CPI-IW** (industrial workers) | **Labour Bureau** | **2016** (from September 2020); earlier 1960 → 1982 → **2001** | **Dearness allowance** of government employees
CPI-AL / CPI-RL | Labour Bureau | 1986-87 | Agricultural and rural labour wages
**WPI** | **Office of Economic Adviser, DPIIT, Ministry of Commerce and Industry** | **2011-12** (from May 2017); 2004-05 series released 14 September 2010 | Goods only; **manufactured products** have the largest weight (about 64%)
**Core inflation** | - | - | Headline **minus food and fuel**
**PPI** | Proposed | - | **Average change** in prices received by producers
@end

The WPI working group on the 2004-05 series was chaired by **Abhijit Sen** (2007). The **inflation target** is **4% ± 2%** (CPI), set under the RBI Act amendment of 2016.

